;;; A query's class prints a pattern inside a pattern as written — M1f
;;; **W54**, register row `W54/goal-nested-var` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`),
;;; filed by S1f.5.7 (T1f.5.7.1, 2026-11-09) from its step 1, `open` and owned
;;; by S1f.5.7: found where `(not (eq ?x ?y))` in a goal printed its class.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two stored negations, `(not (p a))` and `(not (p b))`, and a query whose
;;; goal is `(not (p ?x))`, with the claim that its one class is the two.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The goal has two rows, `?x = a` and `?x = b`, so the class is the goal's
;;; two instances, `(not (p a))` and `(not (p b))` (`region_language.md` § 5),
;;; and the claim holds: exit 0.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The class grounds a conjunct's arguments one level deep, and prints a
;;; pattern inside it as written: the two rows give one fact, `(not (p ?x))`,
;;; the class is `(model (not (p ?x)))`, which no claim can state, and the
;;; claim is FAILED, exit 1 (`ein solve FILE` at `dc4c0bdc`, release and debug
;;; alike).

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a node")
(not (p a))
(not (p b))
(query :goal (not (p ?x)) :expect (model (not (p a)) (not (p b))))
